The 1949 death of James Forrestal, the first U.S. Secretary of Defense, remains a major focus of conspiracy theories because of highly unusual physical evidence, sudden changes in security, and decades of government secrecy. While historians conclude he tragically succumbed to severe depression and paranoia, several anomalies fueled theories that he was murdered to prevent him from leaking government secrets:1. The Dressing Gown Sash and "Hanging" TheoryThe Knot: Forrestal fell from a 16th-floor kitchen window across the hall from his room. When his body was found, the sash of his dressing gown was tightly knotted around his neck.The Discrepancy: The official narrative suggested he tied one end to a radiator to hang himself, but the knot slipped, causing him to fall. Skeptics argue it makes little sense to try to hang yourself out of a high window, suggesting instead that the sash was used to strangle or restrain him during a struggle.2. Evidence of a StruggleBroken Glass: Unreleased photographs and findings from the Navy's investigation showed broken glass on Forrestal’s bed and photographed on the floor of his room.Scrape Marks: There were reported scrape marks on the outside of the window sill, which skeptics interpret as signs of Forrestal desperately clawing to hold on after being pushed.3. Suspicious Security LapsesThe Guard Swapped: Forrestal was under 24-hour suicide watch. However, on the night of his death, his regular guard was unexpectedly replaced by a new guard who left Forrestal completely unattended to go on an errand.Unlocked Window: Forrestal was intentionally placed on a high floor of the Bethesda Naval Hospital for his protection, yet the kitchen window across the hall was left entirely unlocked and unguarded.4. The Unfinished PoemThe Note: Forrestal was in the middle of copying a bleak poem, Chorus from Ajax by Sophocles, which muses on the comfort of death.The Interruption: The transcription stopped mid-word ("night-"). Conspiracy theorists argue he didn't write it as a suicide note, but rather that he was actively writing when someone entered the room and interrupted him. Furthermore, handwriting experts have disputed whether the text was even written in Forrestal's handwriting.5. Immediate Confiscation of Personal PapersThe Diaries: Immediately following his death (and even shortly before his hospitalization), the U.S. government confiscated his personal diaries.White House Lockup: Fifteen loose-leaf binders totaling roughly 3,000 pages were removed from the Pentagon and locked in the White House by the Truman administration. When censored versions were finally published years later, large sections were entirely missing.6. Decades of Extreme SecrecyThe Withheld Report: The official Navy review board (the Willcutts Report) curiously did not explicitly conclude that Forrestal committed suicide, only that the fall killed him.55-Year Delay: The U.S. government refused to release the full investigation report to the public for 55 years, finally unsealing it in 2004. This half-century of silence heavily intensified rumors of a massive cover-up.The Context of the MotiveIn UFO and Majestic 12 lore, the motive for his alleged murder was that his mental breakdown made him a liability, and he was planning to tell the public about the Roswell crash or alien visitations. In political conspiracy circles, it is often argued that he was silenced by geopolitical rivals due to his fierce, public opposition to the creation of Israel and his extreme anti-Soviet paranoia.

